The combined effect of a particle’s speed and its temperature is most commonly represented by its **impact kinetic‑energy (or specific kinetic‑energy)**:

\[
E_{\text{kin}} = \frac12 m v^{2}
\qquad\text{(often expressed per unit volume or mass, i.e., J mm⁻³ or J kg⁻¹)}
\]

In cold‑spray models this parameter captures how the particle’s velocity (which provides the mechanical work needed for adiabatic shear and bonding) and its temperature (which reduces material strength and raises deformability) together determine whether a particle will deform, adhere, and contribute to coating quality. It is therefore used as a predictive metric for coating density, adhesion strength, and microstructure in cold‑sprayed deposits.
